        "card_type": "vocab",
        "identifier": "ID",
        "info": "In Korean, there isn't a direct equivalent to the infinitive verb form as found in some European languages. Instead, the base form of a verb in Korean serves a similar purpose. The base form of a verb, also known as the \"dictionary form,\" is the unconjugated form of the verb that you can find in a Korean dictionary.\n\nForming the Base Form:\nThe base form of regular verbs in Korean usually ends in -다 (-da). For example:\n\n가다 (gada) - to go\n먹다 (meokda) - to eat\n자다 (jada) - to sleep\nIrregular verbs may have different endings in their base form, and there are various irregularities in Korean verb conjugation that need to be memorized.\n\nUsage of the Base Form:\nInfinitive Purpose: Similar to the infinitive in other languages, the base form is used to express the dictionary meaning or purpose of the verb. For instance:\n\n음악을 듣다 (eumak-eul deud-a) - to listen to music\nConjugation Basis: Korean verbs are conjugated based on the stem of the verb, which is often the base form. Different suffixes are added to the base form to create various verb forms, such as past tense, future tense, and more.\n\nFormal Politeness Level:\nThe base form is typically used in informal and plain speech, especially in casual conversations with friends, family, or those of the same or younger age. In formal speech or when addressing someone older or in a higher social position, the polite verb forms would be used, which involve additional polite suffixes.\n\nExample Sentences:\nInfinitive Purpose:\n\n여행을 가다 (yeohaeng-eul gada) - to travel\n음식을 만들다 (eumsik-eul mandeulda) - to cook\nConjugation Basis:\n\n노래를 부르다 (nolae-reul bureuda) - to sing a song (base form used for conjugation)\nKeep in mind that Korean verbs undergo various changes when conjugated based on factors like tense, formality level, and sentence structure. The base form is a fundamental aspect of Korean verbs, serving as the starting point for conjugations and providing the basic meaning of the verb.",
        "native_language": "english",
        "learning_language": "korean",
        "native_group": "3 tourist dialogue 3",
        "native_answer": "leave on a trip",
